ectly verified across a real reboot. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete for node1: the healthy pre-restart boot ID 4d842549-003e-4f78-bb26-12732f129e58 was recorded, the reboot was acknowledged, the post-restart ID changed to 11ba681e-75d9-41b8-87dd-424042931598, and the system returned to running with no failed units and both hardened mounts recovered. Fixed lifecycle boot observations independently corroborate the reboot.","limitations":["nosuid and nodev were verified through live mount flags before and after reboot, but their kernel behavior was not separately exercised with a setuid executable or device node. The live flags are nevertheless direct evidence of the requested mount semantics.","Lifecycle snapshots do not inventory /etc, so retention of /etc/fstab.pre-mount-hardening is inferred directly from its creation and the absence of any later removal command."]}
